Lab 1 Report  Local AI Engine Setup & Python Integration 
 
 
Submitted By:          Cartoneros, Beomarc Andrew D. 
                                   Escabal, Axeil Achia, M. 
 
Submitted To: Mr. Roberto Malitao 
 
 
 
1. CLI Output  Ollama Running Qwen 2.5 
Screenshot below shows the model loaded and responding directly from the Ollama CLI (ollama run qwen2.5:3b), 
confirming the model is active locally. 
 
 
Figure 1. Ollama CLI session  Qwen 2.5:3b responding to a local prompt. 
 
2. Terminal Output  Successful Script Execution 
Screenshot below shows app_verification.py executing successfully, connecting to the local Ollama daemon at 
localhost:11434 and receiving a generated response from Qwen with a recorded inference time. 
 
 
Figure 2. PowerShell terminal  app_verification.py output (Status Code: 200 OK - OPERATIONAL). 
 
 
3. CPU & RAM Utilization During Inference 
Task Manager was monitored during script execution. The inference workload is handled by the llama-server process (the 
runtime Ollama spawns to serve the loaded model), which was consuming approximately 1,965.3 MB of RAM at the time of 
capture, out of 67% total system memory usage. 

 
Figure 3. Windows Task Manager  Processes tab showing llama-server resource usage during inference. 
 
 
Metric Observed Value 
Process llama-server (Ollama inference runtime) 
Peak RAM Usage \uf0bb 1,965.3 MB (~1.9 GB) 
CPU Usage (llama-server) 0% (snapshot after generation completed) 
Total System Memory Usage 67% 
Disk Activity 1% 
Network Activity 0% (confirms fully offline / local inference) 
4. Post-Lab Reflection Questions 
Q1. Architectural Comparison  What happens if you disable your internet connection while running 
app_verification.py? Why does it behave this way compared to proprietary APIs like OpenAI or Gemini? 
Disabling the internet has no effect on app_verification.py  the script still connects and Qwen still generates a 
response, because Ollama hosts the model weights and runs inference entirely on local hardware (CPU/GPU). The 
Python script only talks to localhost:11434 and never leaves the machine. Proprietary APIs like OpenAI or Gemini, 
by contrast, require an active connection because the model weights and compute live on remote cloud servers; each 
request is sent over HTTPS to the provider's data center and a response returned. Without internet, those calls would 
time out or fail. This is the core reason local inference engines like Ollama suit privacy-sensitive, regulated 
environments such as healthcare and finance. 
Q2. Resource Management  What was the peak memory (RAM) usage during Qwen inference on your 
workstation? How would upgrading to a larger model parameter (e.g., qwen2.5:7b or qwen2.5:14b) impact 
hardware requirements? 
Peak RAM usage during inference was approximately 1,965.3 MB (~1.9 GB), consumed by the llama-server 
process while qwen2.5:3b was loaded and generating tokens (see Figure 3). A larger parameter model would 
significantly raise hardware requirements, since RAM/VRAM usage scales roughly with parameter count and 
quantization level: a 7B model would need on the order of 56 GB, and a 14B model roughly 1012 GB or more, with 
slower inference and higher sustained CPU/GPU load. This means bigger models demand more system RAM, a GPU 
with sufficient VRAM, and faster storage, trading resource cost for improved reasoning quality. 
Q3. Security Analysis  Explain how local execution with Ollama prevents data leakage in enterprise 
environments handling confidential customer data. 
With Ollama, the model and all inference computation reside entirely on the local machine or an organization's own 
on-premise servers. Customer prompts, healthcare records, or financial details are processed in local memory and 
never packaged into an outbound request to a third-party cloud provider. As shown in Figure 3, network activity stayed 
at 0% during inference, confirming no data left the machine. This eliminates the main data-leakage risks of cloud LLM 

APIs: interception in transit, third-party logging or retention of prompts, and exposure to breaches at the vendor's 
infrastructure. For industries bound by GDPR or HIPAA, this local-first architecture keeps protected data entirely within 
the organization's own security and audit boundary  essential for compliance.'
